{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I understand you've been having nausea and vomiting since yesterday?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it started after a luncheon. I ate some potato salad and then couldn’t stop vomiting—every 15 minutes or so."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you mentioned some blood streaks in the vomit?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Just a little, but not much. It scared me. I’ve had this before with my gastroparesis, but it’s never this bad this fast."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve reviewed your history. You have gastroparesis after a fundoplication, and you’re on a GJ tube for feeding. That’s correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. I get these flares often. I tried taking Zofran and Ativan at home, but I couldn’t keep anything down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s why we’re admitting you—for IV fluids and medications since you can’t tolerate anything by mouth right now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Is this just another flare, or is something else going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a CT scan to check. No bowel obstruction, no pancreatitis. Your GJ tube is in place, and your SMA dissection is stable—no changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:52:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I was worried about that artery issue. But why the blood in my vomit?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:54:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You had an EGD recently that showed esophagitis and a small ulcer. That’s likely the source. No active bleeding now, and your hemoglobin is stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:56:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, right. I forgot about that. So we’re just waiting for the nausea to pass?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2181-04-30 00:58:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’re giving IV fluids, and we’ve started your usual meds—Zofran, Reglan, and a little Ativan for nausea relief."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2181-04-30 01:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my potassium? I always run low when I’m sick."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2181-04-30 01:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your potassium was 3.6, so we gave IV replacement. It’s now 3.9, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2181-04-30 01:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. And my acidosis? I remember last time I had that too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2181-04-30 01:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, mild anion gap acidosis with low bicarbonate and ketones in your urine. It’s likely from not eating—starvation ketosis. We’re correcting it with fluids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2181-04-30 01:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I haven’t eaten much the past few days. I just don’t feel like it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2181-04-30 01:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s common with gastroparesis flares. We’ll keep you on bowel rest today and monitor your labs tomorrow morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2181-04-30 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your repeat labs look much better. Bicarbonate is now 23, so the acidosis has resolved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2181-04-30 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. I’m still spitting up saliva, but no vomiting since last night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2181-04-30 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good sign. We’re going to start your tube feeds again at a low rate, as recommended by nutrition."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2181-04-30 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that Isosource again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2181-04-30 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, full strength at 15 mL per hour, increasing slowly. We’ll watch for any nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2181-04-30 12:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The enteral feeding started at 12, and you’re tolerating it well so far."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2181-04-30 12:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I don’t feel any worse. That’s a win."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2181-04-30 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to keep you another day to make sure the feeds stay stable and you remain symptom-free."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2181-04-30 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s fine. I’d rather be safe. Any other test results?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2181-04-30 15:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The CT showed a small 12 mm nodule on your left adrenal gland. It’s unchanged from before, but you should follow up with your PCP."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2181-04-30 15:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2181-04-30 15:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not urgent. It’s been stable, so it’s likely benign, but your doctor should check it periodically."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2181-04-30 15:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll make an appointment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2181-05-01 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. Your labs are all stable. Potassium 3.9, bicarbonate 23. You’ve had no nausea, and the feeds are at 25 mL/hr."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2181-05-01 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel much better. Can I go home today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2181-05-01 07:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, if you keep doing well. We’ll discharge you later this morning. We’ll send you home with your usual meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2181-05-01 07:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I restart my citalopram? I missed it yesterday."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2181-05-01 07:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’re restarting it today. Also, your levothyroxine and PPI are continuing as before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2181-05-01 07:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the tube feeds? Should I increase the rate at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2181-05-01 07:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, increase by 10 mL every 6 hours as tolerated, up to 45 mL/hr. Call GI if you have nausea or pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2181-05-01 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All set. You’re cleared to go home. Keep following up with your GI doctor and PCP."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2181-05-01 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’ll take it slow and watch my symptoms."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2181-05-01 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Call us if vomiting returns or you can’t keep feeds down. Otherwise, see you in clinic."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2181-04-30 00:34:00",
    "admission_end": "2181-05-01 15:15:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of gastroparesis following fundoplication was admitted for a one-day episode of severe nausea, vomiting, and inability to tolerate oral intake. She reported bloody streaks in her vomit, but examination and labs showed no active bleeding, with stable hemoglobin and prior findings of esophagitis and esophageal ulcer on recent EGD. CT scan ruled out bowel obstruction and showed stable SMA dissection and a small, unchanged adrenal nodule. She presented with anion gap metabolic acidosis and mild hypokalemia, both resolved with IV fluids and repletion. Bowel rest, IV antiemetics, and gradual restart of enteral feeds led to symptom resolution. She was discharged home on her prior medications with instructions to advance tube feeds and follow up with her PCP regarding the adrenal nodule.",
    "problems": [
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Acidosis",
      "Hypopotassemia",
      "Esophagitis, unspecified",
      "Ulcer of esophagus with bleeding",
      "Status of other artificial opening of gastrointestinal tract",
      "Gastrostomy status",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Dysthymic disorder",
      "Irritable bowel syndrome"
    ]
  }
}
